== Bad Examples

=== Latex

Latex is probably one of the best negative example of bad documentation, which is kind of ironic since it is tool that is widely used in the academic field to write documentations and scientifc papers. Latex suffers from what I call the "bad discoverability problem". Yes, there are documentations about how to use latex, but it's unbelibable hard to find them and there are often not in a useable format.

Let's give an example: Let's assume I am an author who wants to use latex for my work. This means I'm looking for a Low-Level Documentation, probably a tutoral to get started and an API reference to use to look up certain features. 

The current process to find these things go as follows (2025 using the official latex website). As an example, let's simply assume I want to find out how sections (headings) work when writing in latex.

+ Open up the latex website
+ Find the link to the documentation page (either in the footer or via help/documentation)
+ Scroll to the section "General documentation" where there are two files:
+ Open up "LaTeX2e for authors — new features"
+ Look at the content: It looks very technical, but doesn't seem to mention sections. Maybe its in the other document
+ Open up "LaTeX2e for authors — historic version"
+ Look at the content: This one looks more promising, but using the browser search feature doesn't yield any usefull results for "section".
+ Let's read through it in more detail...
+ Aha, under section 1.4. further information, there's an information that you should read "LATEX: A Document Preparation System by Leslie Lamport" for an introduction to latex.
+ Let's follow the reference, looks like we need to buy a book first... maybe we can find it online.
+ Google "LATEX: A Document Preparation System by Leslie Lamport", luckily there's a PDF online
+ Open up the pdf, looks like its a photoscann of the book, so we can't use the browsers search feature
+ Look at the table of content: Aha, under section 2.2.3 Sectioning might be what I'm looking for
+ Indeed, here I can find the information I was looking for